WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.000
A serious server-side failure can occur
when an application accepts a well-formed

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.909
value that the client had no authority to
determine.

3
00:00:07.989 --> 00:00:11.653
Price, discount, balance, tax, shipping
cost, subscription tier,

4
00:00:11.733 --> 00:00:15.912
entitlement, approval status, inventory,
transaction limits, ownership,

5
00:00:15.992 --> 00:00:20.492
timestamps, and administrative flags are
not ordinary input when the server has an

6
00:00:20.572 --> 00:00:21.858
authoritative source.

7
00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:25.088
Validation can prove that unitPrice is a
positive number;

8
00:00:25.168 --> 00:00:28.125
it cannot prove that the number is the
permitted price.

9
00:00:28.205 --> 00:00:31.162
Define the request around choices the
client may make.

10
00:00:31.242 --> 00:00:34.907
For an order, that may be product ID,
quantity, and shipping option.

11
00:00:34.987 --> 00:00:38.394
The server retrieves authoritative product
and account data,

12
00:00:38.474 --> 00:00:42.331
checks availability and entitlements,
calculates the current price,

13
00:00:42.411 --> 00:00:43.761
and persists the result.

14
00:00:43.841 --> 00:00:47.055
A submitted total can help detect a stale
checkout display,

15
00:00:47.135 --> 00:00:49.385
but it must not become the amount charged.

16
00:00:49.465 --> 00:00:52.423
The same model applies to workflow and
feature access:

17
00:00:52.503 --> 00:00:57.131
the client requests an action, while
server-owned rules derive the resulting state.

18
00:00:57.211 --> 00:00:59.654
Mass assignment, also called over-posting,

19
00:00:59.734 --> 00:01:04.363
breaks this boundary by copying an
arbitrary request body into a persistence update.

20
00:01:04.443 --> 00:01:06.950
The visible form may expose only
displayName,

21
00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:11.016
yet a direct request can add role,
tenantId, accountBalance, or isAdmin.

22
00:01:11.096 --> 00:01:15.403
Spreading that body into a database update
turns undocumented properties into

23
00:01:15.483 --> 00:01:16.319
capabilities.

24
00:01:16.399 --> 00:01:18.778
The safer pattern is an explicit
allowlist:

25
00:01:18.858 --> 00:01:23.165
construct an update object from the small
set of validated fields the operation

26
00:01:23.245 --> 00:01:25.816
permits, and set ownership, privilege,
audit,

27
00:01:25.896 --> 00:01:28.982
and derived values from trusted context or
server logic.

28
00:01:29.062 --> 00:01:33.819
Separate input models from database
entities so transport shapes cannot accidentally

29
00:01:33.899 --> 00:01:35.571
become persistence commands.

30
00:01:35.651 --> 00:01:39.508
Sensitive operations may deserve
purpose-specific service methods:

31
00:01:39.588 --> 00:01:41.902
changing a profile name, assigning a role,

32
00:01:41.982 --> 00:01:46.675
and transferring ownership should travel
through different authorization and audit

33
00:01:46.755 --> 00:01:47.141
paths.

34
00:01:47.221 --> 00:01:51.335
Reviewers can then see which
caller-controlled values enter an operation,

35
00:01:51.415 --> 00:01:55.273
which values the server derives, and which
invariants precede a write.

36
00:01:55.353 --> 00:01:59.981
The server remains authoritative by
limiting client input to decisions the client is

37
00:02:00.061 --> 00:02:01.540
actually entitled to make.
